<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John VI Kantakouzenos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1292–<a href="/years/1383">1383</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John VI Kantakouzenos was a Byzantine statesman who fought the civil war of 1341–1347 against the regency for John V Palaiologos, relying on Turkish allies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umur-bey">Umur Bey</a> of Aydin and the Saruhanids. He ruled as emperor from 1347 to 1354, made his son Manuel despot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a>, and after abdicating became a monk and historian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-vi-kantakouzenos.jpg" alt="John VI Kantakouzenos" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uploaded by Cplakidas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_VI_Kantakouzenos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Kantakouzenos was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, the son of Michael Kantakouzenos, governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a>. Through his mother he was related to the reigning Palaiologos house, and through his wife Irene Asanina to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-iii-palaiologos">Andronikos III Palaiologos</a>. He supported Andronikos III against his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos">Andronikos II Palaiologos</a> and, after the accession in 1328, served as grand domestic. When Andronikos III died in June 1341, Kantakouzenos became regent for the nine-year-old John V Palaiologos. By his own account he had refused several times to be crowned co-emperor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In September 1341, while Kantakouzenos was away raising an army, the Patriarch John XIV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and Alexios Apokaukos overthrew his regency. His relatives were exiled or imprisoned and his mother died under house arrest. His army proclaimed him emperor at Didymoteichon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, and he accepted while still styling himself the junior ruler.</p><p>The civil war lasted six years and nearly ruined the empire. Kantakouzenos fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> sheltered him, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> later changed sides, and Umur of Aydin twice rescued him. He then allied with the Ottoman ruler Orhan Bey, giving him his daughter Theodora in marriage. Kantakouzenos entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 8 February 1347, agreeing that he would rule as senior emperor for ten years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His reign was one of continuing decline. The Genoese fortified their colony at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galata">Galata</a> and took as much as 87 per cent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a> revenue. Kantakouzenos built a fleet to break their hold, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> destroyed or captured it by early 1349. In 1351 he oversaw the Fifth Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, which declared the hesychastic theology of Gregory Palamas Orthodox. In 1354 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a>, their first foothold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, as part payment of his debts.</p><p>John V removed him from power, and he became a monk on 10 December 1354 under the name Joasaph Christodoulos. In retirement he wrote a four-volume History of the years 1320–1356, an apologia for his actions and the only surviving account by a Byzantine emperor of his own reign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John VI Kantakouzenos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dušan also gave sanctuary to <b>John VI Kantakouzenos</b>, the former Byzantine regent then in revolt, and allied with him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor <b>John VI Kantakouzenos</b> then made terms with Genoa and left the War of the Straits.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-13">13 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The civil war fought over the guardianship of the young John V Palaiologos, between the regency of Anna of Savoy and <b>John VI Kantakouzenos</b>, ended in a power-sharing agreement.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-8">8 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The army and supporters of <b>John VI Kantakouzenos</b>, largely drawn from the land-holding aristocracy, proclaimed him emperor, effectively starting a civil war against the regency for the boy John V Palaiologos.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-26">26 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Mehmed's son Umur Bey built a fleet at Smyrna and raided the Aegean islands, Greece and the Black Sea, and as an ally of <b>John VI Kantakouzenos</b> fought in the Byzantine civil war.</blockquote><a href="/state/aydinids">Aydinids</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1349 <b>John VI Kantakouzenos</b> made his son Manuel Kantakouzenos despot of the province, giving it a permanent ruler.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-the-morea">Despotate of the Morea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mystras</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1351" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John VI Kantakouzenos"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4934 4064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4934 4064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1351">What was here in 1351? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
